    "title": "pandas.Index.copy — pandas 3.1.0.dev0+1662.g0c49719283 documentation",
    "status": 200,
    "markdown": "# pandas.Index.copy#\n\n- \nIndex.copy(*name=None* ,*deep=False* )[source]#\n- Make a copy of this object. Name is set on the new object. \n  - Parameters:\n    - **name** Label, optional\n    - Set name for new object.\n    - **deep** bool, default False\n      - If True attempts to make a deep copy of the Index.\n      - Else makes a shallow copy.\n  - Returns:\n    - Index\n    - Index refer to new object which is a copy of this object.\n See also \n  - `Index.delete`\n  - Make new Index with passed location(-s) deleted.\n  - `Index.drop`\n  - Make new Index with passed list of labels deleted.\n Notes In most cases, there should be no functional difference from using `deep` , but if`deep` is passed it will attempt to deepcopy.Examples >>> idx = pd.Index([\"a\", \"b\", \"c\"]) >>> new_idx = idx.copy() >>> idx is new_idx False",
    "markdown_length": 814,
